 🏎️ Hello, Saturday! Smart Brevity™ count: 1,751 words ... 6½ mins. Thanks to Erica Pandey for orchestrating. Edited by Katie Lewis. 

 🇨🇦 Trade talks between the U.S. and Canada fell apart late last night, and both sides said steep new tariffs will go into effect. It's an unprecedented breakdown in relations with the second-largest U.S. trading partner, Axios' Ben Berkowitz and Courtenay Brown report . 

 🪖 Army Secretary Dan Driscoll is expected to head to the private sector in the coming months after tension with Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth, The Wall Street Journal scoops . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing — Scoop: Rogers-AIPAC rupture roils Michigan Senate race 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Mike Rogers. Photo illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Photo: Sarah Rice/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Allies of former U.S. Rep. Mike Rogers , Michigan's GOP nominee for the U.S. Senate, have asked AIPAC not to advertise on his behalf, triggering a rupture with the powerful but polarizing pro-Israel group, Axios' Alex Isenstadt reports. 

 Rogers spoke with AIPAC chair Michael Tuchin in Los Angeles last week. The next day, AIPAC shelved an already-cut video ad targeting Rogers' Democratic rival, outspoken Israel critic Abdul El-Sayed. Why it matters: The extraordinary tension underscores how far AIPAC's political standing has fallen amid voters' anger over the war in Gaza. 

 Even Rogers — a staunchly pro-Israel Republican — fears the group's public support could be a liability in the Nov. 3 election. ⚡ Driving the news: The move by Rogers' team angered AIPAC officials, who had spent more than $30 million to try to defeat El-Sayed in the Democratic primary and prepared to spend millions more to help elect Rogers, according to people familiar with the conversations.

 Zoom out: El-Sayed made AIPAC a centerpiece of his successful primary campaign, using the group's spending against him to portray Democratic opponent Haley Stevens as beholden to the pro-Israel lobby.

 El-Sayed has called Israel a "rogue state," accused it of genocide and apartheid, and has declined to say whether Israel has a right to exist as a Jewish state. That made defeating him a priority for AIPAC, which rarely mounts major spending campaigns in general elections. 👀 Behind the scenes: Rogers' team warned AIPAC before the Aug. 4 Democratic primary that it believed El-Sayed would beat Stevens, though AIPAC held out hope Stevens would prevail. The two sides agreed to keep talking.

 After El-Sayed narrowly won, AIPAC told Rogers' allies it wanted to continue its advertising offensive into the Nov. 3 general election. But Rogers' team believed AIPAC's primary ads had backfired by giving El-Sayed a powerful foil — one he was eager to use again. "AIPAC, if you're listening, come back and burn it again," El-Sayed said before his primary victory. Rogers' allies urged AIPAC to get involved without putting the group's name front and center, people familiar with the talks told Axios.

 One option: Directing AIPAC donors to a pro-Rogers super PAC instead of the group running its own ads, or a joint fundraising group the Rogers campaign has established with the national GOP. Rogers' team also proposed hiring AIPAC's political strategists to help guide its effort. But AIPAC officials weren't satisfied: The group wanted a visible role in defeating El-Sayed and argued that because Rogers is strongly pro-Israel, El-Sayed would tie him to the pro-Israel lobbying group regardless. 🔎 State of play: Direct talks between Rogers' team and AIPAC have now gone cold, prompting a broader GOP effort to broker peace. Jewish Republican donors with ties to both sides are acting as intermediaries, people familiar with the discussions said . 

 Some national Republicans now worry that AIPAC — whose super PAC ended July with nearly $60 million in the bank — will sit out the general election entirely. "I welcome any and all support from the pro-Israel community, and I'm proud to have their involvement," Rogers told Axios.

 AIPAC representatives didn't respond to requests for comment. Share this story . 

 
 
