 3. ✨ Wall Street&#x27;s AI blind spot 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 There's a growing disconnect between the business and politics of data centers, Axios' Dan Primack writes. 

 The business is booming. Public and private equities are both up and to the right — not only for actual project operators, but also for related energy and vendor plays. The politics have turned sharply negative , often on a bipartisan basis . A growing number of states have implemented or are considering moratoriums and bans. 🪧 Behind the scenes: Data center investors lament the opposition, often framing it as a correctable community education issue. They also believe that much of the political pushback will dissipate after the midterm elections.

 But Wall Street and Silicon Valley aren't the electorate. "We can't let China win the AI race" isn't a salient statement for most normies, particularly when their electricity bills keep rising. A Wall Street executive active in the sector told us: "There is a time horizon problem. ... Capital is underwriting decades of demand while politics runs to the next election. Those two things at times don't reconcile easily."

